WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:04.760
I'm Jenna Fisher.

2
00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:06.060
And I'm Angela Kinsey.

3
00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:07.340
We were on The Office together.

4
00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:08.600
And we're best friends.

5
00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:12.420
And now we're doing the Ultimate Office Rewatch podcast just for you.

6
00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:19.400
Each week, we will break down an episode of The Office and give exclusive behind-the-scenes stories that only two people who were there can tell you.

7
00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:20.560
We're The Office Ladies.

8
00:00:24.390 --> 00:00:25.130
Hi, lady.

9
00:00:25.590 --> 00:00:26.450
Good morning.

10
00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:30.200
Wow, you're in a chipper mood today.

11
00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:31.440
I'm very chipper.

12
00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:33.440
I mean, I have been up since 8 a.m.

13
00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:38.880
with a plumber, but that didn't stop me from being excited and happy to be here.

14
00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:39.980
I love it.

15
00:00:40.660 --> 00:00:42.720
Unfortunately, Sam has the hiccups.

16
00:00:43.060 --> 00:00:43.860
He does.

17
00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:45.880
Do you have a hiccups cure?

18
00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:46.800
Because I have one.

19
00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:48.620
Hold your breath?

20
00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:49.700
No.

21
00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:50.280
No.

22
00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:54.880
You take 10 teeny tiny sips of water.

23
00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:56.260
Okay.

24
00:00:56.800 --> 00:01:00.500
And then stop and then do it a second time.

25
00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:03.960
But you take the teeniest sip and you swallow it completely.

26
00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:05.900
And then you take another teeny sip.

27
00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:06.760
All right.

28
00:01:06.820 --> 00:01:07.960
I'm doing it now.

29
00:01:08.140 --> 00:01:08.660
Okay.

30
00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:09.940
Let us know.

31
00:01:10.540 --> 00:01:11.220
All right.

32
00:01:11.300 --> 00:01:15.880
Well, while Sam is sipping his tiny sips of water, I'm going to tell you, there it is.

33
00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:17.260
We just heard the hiccup.

34
00:01:17.980 --> 00:01:24.820
We're talking about Season 8, Episode 6, written by Daniel Chun and directed by Troy Miller.

35
00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:26.620
Here is your summary.

36
00:01:26.620 --> 00:01:37.820
Dwight sends a ripple of panic through the Scranton branch when he introduces an accountability tracking device to the office software meant to reduce the number of mistakes being made.

37
00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:45.540
If they make five mistakes, an email will go out to Robert California revealing what the employees really think of him.

38
00:01:45.940 --> 00:01:50.740
Meanwhile, Gabe and Daryl compete for the affection of new warehouse manager, Val.

39
00:01:51.220 --> 00:01:52.460
Sam, how's it going?

40
00:01:52.940 --> 00:01:53.860
He's sipping.

41
00:01:53.860 --> 00:01:55.040
It's still going.

42
00:01:55.340 --> 00:01:55.780
Okay.

43
00:01:56.100 --> 00:01:56.440
All right.

44
00:01:56.660 --> 00:02:02.420
Listen, my only other thing I know about hiccups is that if someone scares me, then they go away.

45
00:02:02.420 --> 00:02:03.720
But I don't recommend it.

46
00:02:03.800 --> 00:02:04.320
It's unpleasant.

47
00:02:06.140 --> 00:02:06.800
All right.

48
00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:08.700
Here is fast fact number one.

49
00:02:08.980 --> 00:02:11.960
I got to speak with writer Danny Chun about this episode.

50
00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:13.260
He is so lovely.

51
00:02:13.540 --> 00:02:20.760
He told me that the core emotion that they were exploring with this episode was Dwight feeling underappreciated.

52
00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:31.880
Oh, Danny said that he really loved episodes that explored Dwight's sensitivity and how his feelings manifest in a kind of twisted and weird way because he's, quote,

53
00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:33.680
incapable of normal expression.

54
00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:40.900
Danny also said that the writers were very aware that Pam is the person who really understands Dwight.

55
00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:45.340
And yeah, they they made sure to have that come out in this episode as well.

56
00:02:45.340 --> 00:02:47.840
So, yeah, I enjoyed all of those scenes.

57
00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:48.700
I always do.

58
00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:58.080
Danny also mentioned that in terms of this season, they were really toggling a lot between who was the subject of the A story with Steve Corral.

59
00:02:58.200 --> 00:02:59.140
It was always Michael Scott.

60
00:02:59.360 --> 00:03:02.240
He was pretty much exclusively our A story.

61
00:03:02.240 --> 00:03:06.260
But with this season, they wanted to lean on different characters.

62
00:03:07.060 --> 00:03:15.100
Danny said one of the benefits of our shooting schedule this season was that the writers were able to figure things out and get kind of feedback in real time.

63
00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:20.720
Like, we weren't that far ahead in terms of shooting an episode, having it air, feeling the feedback.

64
00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:29.360
And the writers liked the fact that they could kind of adjust things in future episodes based on that feedback.

65
00:03:29.900 --> 00:03:32.420
And that was a lot of what they were doing this season.

66
00:03:32.420 --> 00:03:34.180
And he said, so you're going to feel it.

67
00:03:34.340 --> 00:03:43.100
You're going to feel them, like, trying things and then being like, oh, maybe that didn't work or trying things and being like, oh, yes, we found something here.

68
00:03:43.240 --> 00:03:48.060
So I thought that was a really interesting thing to think about just in terms of season eight as a whole.

69
00:03:48.880 --> 00:03:49.320
Absolutely.

70
00:03:49.320 --> 00:03:51.700
I'm going to have that in mind now as we rewatch.

71
00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:59.060
Fast fact number two is all about the song that plays in our cold open this week, Closing Time by Semisonic.

72
00:03:59.480 --> 00:04:02.800
You know, we'll break down the cold open when we get to it.

73
00:04:02.920 --> 00:04:05.320
But this song is a big part of it.

74
00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:08.000
You know, Andy's going to walk around singing this song.

75
00:04:08.140 --> 00:04:09.520
He's closing up the office.

76
00:04:09.860 --> 00:04:13.080
We got a lot of mail about this song, and it was super interesting.

77
00:04:13.260 --> 00:04:14.700
And it's all stuff I did not know.

78
00:04:15.080 --> 00:04:15.880
Let's hear it.

79
00:04:15.880 --> 00:04:18.020
First of all, Kobe S.

80
00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:28.860
from Colfax, Wisconsin, said, Dan Wilson, the main songwriter and frontman for the band Semisonic, wrote the song when his wife was pregnant with their first child.

81
00:04:29.180 --> 00:04:36.220
He said people often think that this song is about being bounced from a bar, but it's actually about being bounced from the womb.

82
00:04:36.920 --> 00:04:37.480
What?

83
00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:38.560
Yeah.

84
00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:40.240
And Jennifer T.

85
00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:49.240
from Milwaukee, Wisconsin, said, I was listening to the podcast Song Exploder, episode 176 from January 2020.

86
00:04:49.740 --> 00:04:56.500
If you aren't familiar, Song Exploder features musicians breaking down a song and telling the story of how a song came to be.

87
00:04:56.720 --> 00:05:00.180
The episode about Closing Time did not disappoint.

88
00:05:00.180 --> 00:05:07.860
My perspective of the song has completely changed since I've learned the background and was surprised how sweet and touching it is.

89
00:05:08.440 --> 00:05:15.940
Jennifer goes on to say, basically, lead singer Dan Wilson had a very premature daughter that had to spend almost a year in the hospital.

90
00:05:16.280 --> 00:05:23.280
The song was written during that time when the doctors encouraged him to take a break from being at the hospital and keep living his life.

91
00:05:23.280 --> 00:05:28.640
And the day they got to take their daughter home, coincidentally, was the day the song was released.

92
00:05:29.100 --> 00:05:29.980
Oh, my goodness.

93
00:05:30.640 --> 00:05:37.820
So I have a couple of audio clips because I thought it would be fun to listen to the song and hear the lyrics with this new information.

94
00:05:38.420 --> 00:05:39.780
It just warms my heart.

95
00:05:39.900 --> 00:05:41.720
Closing Time was like, we're out of this hospital.

96
00:05:42.180 --> 00:05:42.420
Yeah.

97
00:05:43.040 --> 00:05:43.420
All right.

98
00:05:43.560 --> 00:05:45.180
So here is clip number one.

99
00:05:50.720 --> 00:05:59.800
Closing Time, open all the doors and let you out into the world.

100
00:06:01.960 --> 00:06:10.020
Closing Time, turn all of the lights on over every boy and every girl.

101
00:06:12.530 --> 00:06:13.680
Do you kind of hear it?

102
00:06:13.800 --> 00:06:16.720
I know who I want to take me home.

103
00:06:16.860 --> 00:06:18.960
The baby's going home with her mom and dad.

104
00:06:19.200 --> 00:06:19.660
Yes.

105
00:06:20.100 --> 00:06:20.340
Yes.

106
00:06:20.580 --> 00:06:20.740
Okay.

107
00:06:20.820 --> 00:06:21.800
Listen to this next clip.

108
00:06:26.700 --> 00:06:41.320
Time for you to, this room won't be our sister.

109
00:06:45.510 --> 00:06:46.010
See?

110
00:06:46.550 --> 00:06:47.230
The womb.

111
00:06:47.230 --> 00:06:51.810
The womb is not going to be available again until some brothers and sisters come.

112
00:06:52.010 --> 00:06:52.690
Yes.

113
00:06:52.910 --> 00:06:53.670
Because you're leaving.

114
00:06:53.990 --> 00:06:55.010
You're going out.

115
00:06:55.210 --> 00:06:55.650
Closing time.

116
00:06:56.210 --> 00:06:58.290
My vagina shut down.

117
00:06:58.670 --> 00:06:59.290
Yes.

118
00:06:59.670 --> 00:07:00.310
For a bit.

119
00:07:00.590 --> 00:07:01.350
Your uterus.

120
00:07:01.990 --> 00:07:02.410
Uterus.

121
00:07:05.590 --> 00:07:06.270
Yeah.

122
00:07:07.370 --> 00:07:10.810
I mean, I think they both are shut down after childbirth.

123
00:07:10.950 --> 00:07:11.550
Let's face it.

124
00:07:11.770 --> 00:07:12.510
For a while.

125
00:07:12.770 --> 00:07:13.150
For a while.

126
00:07:13.170 --> 00:07:13.390
For a while.

127
00:07:13.390 --> 00:07:13.440
Yeah.

128
00:07:13.510 --> 00:07:14.390
It's a good idea.

129
00:07:15.330 --> 00:07:15.690
Aw.

130
00:07:15.970 --> 00:07:16.850
So sweet.

131
00:07:17.230 --> 00:07:18.190
That's so sweet.

132
00:07:18.470 --> 00:07:19.450
So sweet.

133
00:07:19.810 --> 00:07:24.870
I watched a whole clip of him telling the story during a live concert.

134
00:07:24.870 --> 00:07:29.150
But then I also watched their music video for this song.

135
00:07:29.430 --> 00:07:34.330
And in the music video, they are shutting down what looks like a restaurant or a bar.

136
00:07:34.330 --> 00:07:39.550
So you see why people associate it with being at a bar.

137
00:07:40.130 --> 00:07:40.290
Well, yeah.

138
00:07:40.550 --> 00:07:46.390
But, you know, I imagine that was some creative person that worked for the music video company.

139
00:07:46.390 --> 00:07:54.350
And they were like, listen, we know this is about closing the doors to a hospital and also a uterus slash vagina.

140
00:07:54.350 --> 00:07:58.110
But maybe that is difficult to portray in a music video.

141
00:07:59.070 --> 00:08:00.810
So maybe we switch gears here.

142
00:08:01.070 --> 00:08:03.150
Hear me out and make it about a bar.

143
00:08:04.150 --> 00:08:12.270
Well, Dan Wilson did say that, you know, whenever you're in a band and then like the lead singer has a baby, they have to write a song about their baby.

144
00:08:12.490 --> 00:08:16.730
And the whole band has to go along with it because you can't tell them you can't write a song about your new baby.

145
00:08:16.730 --> 00:08:21.010
And he was like, so I wanted to write a song about it, but I tried to be really clever.

146
00:08:21.290 --> 00:08:23.010
Unfortunately, I was so clever.

147
00:08:23.170 --> 00:08:25.110
No one knows this song is about my baby.

148
00:08:25.650 --> 00:08:26.130
Aw.

149
00:08:27.030 --> 00:08:28.270
Well, now we do.

150
00:08:28.490 --> 00:08:29.710
And we think it's so sweet.

151
00:08:30.190 --> 00:08:30.470
Yes.

152
00:08:30.770 --> 00:08:31.130
Okay.

153
00:08:31.230 --> 00:08:36.130
So now moving to fastback number three, this one is all about the doomsday clock.

154
00:08:36.750 --> 00:08:39.270
This episode is called Doomsday.

155
00:08:39.770 --> 00:08:42.870
I had vaguely heard about the doomsday clock.

156
00:08:43.210 --> 00:08:44.390
Do you know about it, Angela?

157
00:08:44.390 --> 00:08:46.790
Yes, yes, of course, of course.

158
00:08:46.790 --> 00:08:51.170
There's always some announcement where we're at with the doomsday clock, and it's always really depressing.

159
00:08:51.790 --> 00:08:53.110
That's what Lee said.

160
00:08:53.390 --> 00:08:56.650
Like, we're the closest to doomsday ever right now.

161
00:08:56.670 --> 00:08:57.130
We are.

162
00:08:57.330 --> 00:09:00.630
We are at 90 seconds to doomsday.

163
00:09:00.930 --> 00:09:01.250
Yeah.

164
00:09:01.510 --> 00:09:03.810
The clock is set at 90 seconds to midnight.

165
00:09:04.490 --> 00:09:04.630
Yeah.

166
00:09:04.710 --> 00:09:04.910
All right.

167
00:09:04.910 --> 00:09:08.270
So for anyone who doesn't know about the doomsday clock, a.k.a.

168
00:09:08.430 --> 00:09:17.670
me, yesterday, the doomsday clock was designed to warn the public about how close we are to destroying our world with dangerous technologies.

169
00:09:18.050 --> 00:09:18.250
Yes.

170
00:09:18.470 --> 00:09:19.290
Global extinction.

171
00:09:19.850 --> 00:09:20.390
Yeah.

172
00:09:20.390 --> 00:09:23.510
It was created all the way back in 1947.

173
00:09:24.410 --> 00:09:28.510
They thought that the greatest danger to humanity came from nuclear weapons.

174
00:09:28.870 --> 00:09:33.450
This was because the United States and Soviet Union were in their nuclear arms race.

175
00:09:33.450 --> 00:09:34.110
Hmm.

176
00:09:34.610 --> 00:09:46.850
In 1991, with the end of the Cold War, the clock was set at 17 minutes to midnight, and that is the farthest we have ever been from global extinction, was in 1991, I guess.

177
00:09:47.210 --> 00:09:47.430
Hmm.

178
00:09:47.430 --> 00:09:58.350
The decision as to where to set the clock, it's set by a nonprofit called The Bulletin, which was founded by scientists who worked on the Manhattan Project, you know,

179
00:09:58.430 --> 00:10:00.770
the scientists who built the first atomic bombs.

180
00:10:00.770 --> 00:10:08.110
And they realized that they could not remain aloof to the consequences of their work, so they formed this group called The Bulletin.

181
00:10:08.410 --> 00:10:19.210
They have reset the doomsday clock 25 times, most recently in 2023, when we moved it from 100 seconds to midnight to 90 seconds to midnight.

182
00:10:19.550 --> 00:10:20.050
Mm-hmm.

183
00:10:20.430 --> 00:10:29.930
The doomsday clock has been featured in a lot of music, television, movies, clearly not things I listen to or watch because I was pretty ignorant to the doomsday clock.

184
00:10:30.830 --> 00:10:36.530
But there is an Iron Maiden song called Two Minutes to Midnight that is about the doomsday clock.

185
00:10:36.930 --> 00:10:40.410
It was a recurring theme in the Watchmen graphic novels.

186
00:10:40.810 --> 00:10:41.270
Yeah.

187
00:10:41.650 --> 00:10:51.250
And if you want, you can download an entire doomsday clock playlist on Spotify, and they're all songs about the clock and our impending doom.

188
00:10:52.150 --> 00:10:52.570
Yay!

189
00:10:53.010 --> 00:10:53.570
Road trip!

190
00:10:54.690 --> 00:10:55.950
Well, you know what?

191
00:10:56.070 --> 00:10:58.550
You can road trip to see the doomsday clock.

192
00:10:58.550 --> 00:11:03.030
It is located in the Bulletin offices at the University of Chicago.

193
00:11:03.590 --> 00:11:04.770
It's in the lobby.

194
00:11:05.150 --> 00:11:11.350
So fire up your playlist and go take a picture with the doomsday clock before it's all over, everybody.

195
00:11:12.010 --> 00:11:13.170
And that's what I got.

196
00:11:13.170 --> 00:11:19.550
I have a little something, on a lighter note, from my digital clutter.

197
00:11:20.010 --> 00:11:26.830
The week we were filming this episode, Steve Burgess sent out an email to the cast and crew titled,

198
00:11:27.230 --> 00:11:28.770
It's Softball Time Again.

199
00:11:29.110 --> 00:11:30.050
Hi, everyone.

200
00:11:30.050 --> 00:11:36.730
For those who didn't play with us last year, the office softball team participates in the Primetime Softball League.

201
00:11:37.030 --> 00:11:40.330
Games take place in Burbank starting September 10th.

202
00:11:40.790 --> 00:11:42.570
Let us know if you'd like to play.

203
00:11:42.870 --> 00:11:44.590
Playoffs will be at the end of the season.

204
00:11:44.890 --> 00:11:48.570
This league is open to everyone who works on the show and their family members.

205
00:11:48.730 --> 00:11:50.370
Just email or text me.

206
00:11:51.010 --> 00:11:53.110
Angela, I remember the softball league.

207
00:11:53.910 --> 00:11:54.830
Did you play?

208
00:11:55.230 --> 00:11:59.610
I played one year, and then I was pregnant, and then I had a baby.

209
00:11:59.870 --> 00:12:03.110
And then it was very hard to get to softball games.

210
00:12:03.270 --> 00:12:06.610
But I just love, love a company softball team.

211
00:12:06.790 --> 00:12:08.590
It is like one of my favorite things.

212
00:12:09.010 --> 00:12:12.130
And I used to play co-ed softball in my 20s.

213
00:12:12.210 --> 00:12:14.870
With my improv group, we had a softball team.

214
00:12:15.170 --> 00:12:15.710
Guess what?

215
00:12:15.790 --> 00:12:19.810
If you get a bunch of improvisers and they have a softball team, there's lots of bits.

216
00:12:19.810 --> 00:12:21.090
So many bits.

217
00:12:22.170 --> 00:12:24.090
So many bits in the dugout.

218
00:12:24.370 --> 00:12:26.150
I just remember that we had shirts.

219
00:12:26.690 --> 00:12:27.830
Yeah, we had shirts.

220
00:12:28.090 --> 00:12:31.830
That's like half the fun is like going, doing bits.

221
00:12:31.950 --> 00:12:34.230
You get your shirts, and you all go grab a beer after.

222
00:12:34.710 --> 00:12:35.210
Come on.

223
00:12:35.590 --> 00:12:40.170
The softball is like fourth on the list of things that are great about the softball league.

224
00:12:40.550 --> 00:12:41.590
Yeah, exactly.

225
00:12:42.850 --> 00:12:43.430
All right.

226
00:12:43.490 --> 00:12:46.430
Well, I guess we should take a break, and then we will be back.

227
00:12:46.430 --> 00:12:49.830
Sam, do you have a hiccups update for us before the break?

228
00:12:50.630 --> 00:12:52.630
I unfortunately still have the hiccups.

229
00:12:52.790 --> 00:12:54.250
Oh my gosh, Sam.

230
00:12:54.690 --> 00:12:55.230
I know.

231
00:12:55.610 --> 00:12:57.590
Cassie, do you have any ideas for hiccups?

232
00:12:58.450 --> 00:13:02.630
I always just hold my breath for 30 seconds to a minute, and that does it for me.

233
00:13:03.190 --> 00:13:04.590
Yeah, that's where I start.

234
00:13:04.730 --> 00:13:05.910
I start with holding my breath.

235
00:13:06.210 --> 00:13:07.490
Have you done that yet, Sam?

236
00:13:07.830 --> 00:13:09.150
No, I'll hold it right now.

237
00:13:09.310 --> 00:13:10.090
I'll hold it through the break.

238
00:13:10.490 --> 00:13:10.950
All right.

239
00:13:11.050 --> 00:13:12.490
We'll be back in just a second.

240
00:13:24.050 --> 00:13:24.930
We're back.

241
00:13:25.590 --> 00:13:27.330
Sam, how did holding your breath work out?

242
00:13:27.810 --> 00:13:28.530
Not good.

243
00:13:29.050 --> 00:13:29.910
Oh my gosh.

244
00:13:32.130 --> 00:13:34.350
Sam has the most stubborn hiccups ever.

245
00:13:34.610 --> 00:13:35.010
I know.

246
00:13:35.650 --> 00:13:35.930
